{"id":"hemeonc-physiology","system":"Hematology & Oncology","subject":"Physiology","pages":[416,419],"rounds":[{"t":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis and antiglobulin tests","q":[{"s":"During gel electrophoresis, toward which electrode does hemoglobin migrate?","o":["The positively charged anode","Toward both equally","It does not migrate","The negatively charged cathode","Toward the origin only"],"e":["Correct. Hemoglobin moves from the cathode toward the anode.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. It starts at the cathode.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"Which hemoglobin migrates the farthest toward the anode?","o":["HbA","HbH","HbC","HbF","HbS"],"e":["Correct. \"A Fat Santa Claus\": order from anode is A, F, S, C.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Closest to the cathode.","Wrong. Second.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"Which hemoglobin migrates the least distance from the cathode?","o":["HbF","HbC","HbS","HbA2","HbA"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Closest to the cathode.","Wrong. Between F and C.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Farthest."],"a":1,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"Why does HbS migrate more slowly toward the anode than HbA?","o":["Glutamic acid is replaced by lysine","It is larger","Negatively charged glutamic acid is replaced by neutral valine","It lacks heme","It contains γ chains"],"e":["Wrong. HbC.","Wrong. Not the reason.","Correct. HbS is more positively charged.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"The HbC mutation replaces glutamic acid with which amino acid?","o":["Alanine","Aspartate","Lysine","Valine","Glycine"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. A positively charged amino acid, making HbC migrate least.","Wrong. HbS.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"An electrophoresis lane shows bands for HbA and HbF only, in a healthy infant. This represents:","o":["HbC trait","A normal newborn","Sickle cell trait","HbSC disease","Sickle cell disease"],"e":["Wrong. A and C.","Correct.","Wrong. A and S.","Wrong. S and C.","Wrong. S only (with some F)."],"a":1,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"An adult's electrophoresis shows bands for HbA and HbS. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Sickle cell disease","HbC disease","HbSC disease","Sickle cell trait","Normal adult"],"e":["Wrong. No HbA.","Wrong. C only.","Wrong. S and C.","Correct.","Wrong. A only."],"a":3,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"An adult with painful crises has bands at the HbS and HbC positions with no HbA. Diagnosis?","o":["Sickle cell trait","HbSC disease","Normal adult","HbC trait","β-thalassemia minor"],"e":["Wrong. Has HbA.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Has HbA.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"Which mnemonic gives the electrophoresis order from anode to cathode?","o":["\"A Fat Santa Claus can't go far\"","\"Sickle Cells Fly Away\"","\"Can Santa Find Anything\"","\"Always Find Cool Spots\"","\"Fat Cats Sleep Always\""],"e":["Correct. A, F, S, C.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Reverse order.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"What does the direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test detect?","o":["Hemoglobin variants","Platelet antibodies","Complement deficiency","Free antibodies in serum","Antibodies or complement already coated on the patient's RBCs"],"e":["Wrong. Electrophoresis.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Indirect test.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"A 35-year-old woman with SLE has hemolytic anemia and spherocytes. Which test best confirms an autoimmune cause?","o":["Osmotic fragility test","Flow cytometry for CD55/59","Indirect antiglobulin test","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Direct antiglobulin test"],"e":["Wrong. Hereditary spherocytosis.","Wrong. PNH.","Wrong. Pretransfusion testing.","Wrong. Hemoglobinopathies.","Correct. Used for AIHA diagnosis."],"a":4,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"In the indirect antiglobulin test, what is added to the patient's serum?","o":["Thrombin","The patient's own RBCs","Ristocetin","Normal (donor) RBCs, then Coombs reagent","Complement only"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Direct test.","Wrong. Platelet test.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"Before a blood transfusion, which test checks whether the recipient has antibodies that will react with donor RBCs?","o":["Direct antiglobulin test","Bleeding time","Osmotic fragility test","Indirect antiglobulin test","Hemoglobin electrophoresis"],"e":["Wrong. Detects antibodies on the patient's own RBCs.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"A positive direct antiglobulin test shows agglutination because:","o":["Complement is absent","Coombs reagent lyses RBCs","Anti-human globulin cross-links antibody-coated RBCs","The patient's serum has free antibody","The patient's RBCs lack antigens"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Indirect test.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"What reagent is common to both direct and indirect antiglobulin tests?","o":["Donor plasma","Thrombin","Ristocetin","Anti-human globulin (Coombs reagent)","Heparin"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"Which hemoglobin's band lies between HbA and HbS?","o":["Hb Barts","HbA2","HbC","HbH","HbF"],"e":["Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Beyond S.","Wrong. Not listed.","Correct. Order: A, F, S, C."],"a":4,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"A patient's electrophoresis shows a single band at the HbC position. Diagnosis?","o":["HbC trait","HbC disease","Normal","HbSC disease","Sickle cell trait"],"e":["Wrong. Would also have HbA.","Correct. CC.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Would have S.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"A negative direct antiglobulin test in a patient with hemolysis suggests:","o":["The transfusion is incompatible","Warm AIHA is confirmed","Hemoglobin is abnormal","Cold AIHA is confirmed","The hemolysis is not antibody-mediated on the RBC surface"],"e":["Wrong. Indirect test.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not implied.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. No anti-RBC antibody detected."],"a":4,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"},{"s":"Compared with HbA, HbS and HbC are:","o":["More positively charged","Identical in charge","More negatively charged","Unable to bind oxygen","Uncharged"],"e":["Correct. So they migrate less toward the anode.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"416","k":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis"},{"s":"The indirect antiglobulin test is used mainly for:","o":["Measuring iron stores","Diagnosis of hemoglobinopathies","Pretransfusion testing","AIHA diagnosis","Evaluating platelet function"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Electrophoresis.","Correct.","Wrong. Direct test.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"416","k":"Antiglobulin test"}]},{"t":"Platelet plug formation","q":[{"s":"What is the first event after endothelial injury in primary hemostasis?","o":["Platelet aggregation via GpIIb/IIIa","ADP release","Fibrin formation","Plasmin generation","Transient vasoconstriction via neural reflex and endothelin"],"e":["Wrong. Later.","Wrong. After activation.","Wrong. Secondary hemostasis.","Wrong. Fibrinolysis.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Where is vWF stored in endothelial cells?","o":["Specific granules","Weibel-Palade bodies","Dense granules","Azurophilic granules","Lysosomes"],"e":["Wrong. Neutrophils.","Correct. Also stored in platelet α granules.","Wrong. Platelets.","Wrong. Neutrophils.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Platelet adhesion to exposed subendothelial collagen occurs through:","o":["GpIIb/IIIa binding fibrinogen","GpIb binding vWF","CD14 binding LPS","TXA2 receptor","P2Y12 binding ADP"],"e":["Wrong. Aggregation.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Activation."],"a":1,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Binding of ADP to which platelet receptor induces GpIIb/IIIa expression?","o":["PAR-1","TXA2 receptor","P2Y12","GpIb","CD40"],"e":["Wrong. Thrombin.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. vWF.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Which molecule links adjacent platelets during aggregation?","o":["Collagen","vWF via GpIb","Thrombin","ADP","Fibrinogen via GpIIb/IIIa"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Adhesion.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Which factor promotes platelet aggregation?","o":["TXA2 released by platelets","Protein C","PGI2 from endothelium","Heparin","Nitric oxide"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Anticoagulant.","Wrong. Anti-aggregation.","Wrong. Anticoagulant.","Wrong. Anti-aggregation."],"a":0,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Which endothelial products inhibit platelet aggregation?","o":["Calcium and ADP","TXA2 and ADP","Endothelin and serotonin","PGI2 and NO","vWF and fibrinogen"],"e":["Wrong. Pro-hemostatic.","Wrong. Pro-aggregation.","Wrong. Vasoconstrictors.","Correct. They increase blood flow and decrease aggregation.","Wrong. Pro-hemostatic."],"a":3,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"How does aspirin inhibit platelet function?","o":["Irreversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase, blocking TXA2 synthesis","Activates antithrombin","Blocks GpIIb/IIIa directly","Inhibits vWF release","Blocks P2Y12"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Eptifibatide.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Clopidogrel."],"a":0,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor act by:","o":["Blocking the P2Y12 ADP receptor","Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase","Activating plasminogen","Inhibiting cyclooxygenase","Blocking GpIb"],"e":["Correct. Prevents ADP-induced GpIIb/IIIa expression.","Wrong. Warfarin.","Wrong. tPA.","Wrong. Aspirin.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Which drugs inhibit GpIIb/IIIa directly?","o":["Aspirin and NSAIDs","Heparin and LMWH","Clopidogrel and ticagrelor","Desmopressin","Eptifibatide and tirofiban"],"e":["Wrong. COX.","Wrong. Antithrombin.","Wrong. P2Y12.","Wrong. Releases vWF.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Ristocetin causes platelet agglutination by:","o":["Inhibiting COX","Binding fibrinogen directly","Stimulating TXA2 synthesis","Activating vWF to bind GpIb","Activating P2Y12"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"Failure of agglutination in the ristocetin assay occurs in which two conditions?","o":["Glanzmann thrombasthenia and ITP","Vitamin K deficiency and liver disease","DIC and TTP","von Willebrand disease and Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Hemophilia A and B"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Defective vWF or GpIb.","Wrong. Coagulation factor defects."],"a":3,"p":"417","k":"Platelet plug formation"},{"s":"How does desmopressin help patients with mild von Willebrand disease?","o":["It blocks P2Y12","It promotes release of vWF and factor VIII from endothelial cells","It replaces factor IX","It increases platelet production","It inhibits fibrinolysis"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Tranexa